          "heading": "Headway, spacing and stochastic arrivals",
          "content": "## Lesson 1.3: Headway, spacing and stochastic arrivals\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nHeadway is the time between successive vehicle reference points at a fixed station. Spacing is their longitudinal separation at an instant. In a homogeneous steady stream, mean headway approximates the inverse rate; variation in headway contains important information about platooning, capacity and conflict exposure.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nh\u0304 = 3600/q in seconds if q is veh/h/lane.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nA lane with 1,200 vehicles/h has a mean headway of 3.0 seconds. Individual headways might range from less than two seconds to much longer than five seconds; reporting only the mean conceals the critical tail and the distribution of arrivals.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nCompare the implications of identical average headways for a randomly arriving stream and for vehicles released from an upstream traffic signal.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nA Poisson arrival assumption is not appropriate where signal control imposes regular platoons or where queues meter departures.\n\n### Measurement protocol\n\nSpecify which observations distinguish the proposed engineering relationship from a coincidental correlation. Record station location, observation interval, vehicles or users included, calibration checks and missing data. A correct equation evaluated on a biased dataset can create a less defensible conclusion than a rough estimate accompanied by an explicit uncertainty interval.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 1.3.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a busy intersection surrounded by stores, loading areas and narrow footpaths. The primary affected users include local businesses, cyclists and walkers. Before transferring the relationship h\u0304 = 3600/q in seconds if q is veh/h/lane. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for conflicting curb use and vehicle queues; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on reassignment of curb space with inclusive access. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 1.3.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: A lane with 1,200 vehicles/h has a mean headway of 3.0 seconds. Individual headways might range from less than two seconds to much longer than five seconds; reporting only the mean conceals the critical tail and the distribution of arrivals. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 1.3.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: A Poisson arrival assumption is not appropriate where signal control imposes regular platoons or where queues meter departures. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of headway, spacing and stochastic arrivals. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (h\u0304 = 3600/q in seconds if q is veh/h/lane.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about headway, spacing and stochastic arrivals, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
