          "id": "sec-1-9",
          "secNumber": "1.9",
          "heading": "Extended field case: why a road count cannot measure access by itself",
          "content": "## Extended field case: why a road count cannot measure access by itself\n\nConsider a six-kilometre urban road with several schools, a produce market, two bus routes and a railway crossing. A traditional traffic survey might report only a peak-hour vehicle count. That statistic cannot reveal the number of people delivered to work or school, the difficulty of crossing the carriageway or the consequences of a disabled bus in the sole bus lane. A credible first-day survey separates vehicle flow by mode and direction and counts bus occupancy using a sample with visible sampling times. It also observes the path and waiting time of pedestrians. Road-user categories must be defined before surveying; otherwise the resulting counts cannot be reconciled.\n\nConstruct two fifteen-minute windows, one before and one after an afternoon school dismissal. For each, record passenger cars, motorcycles, buses, heavy goods vehicles and bicycles. If one fifteen-minute interval records 180 cars, 55 motorcycles, 12 buses and 7 heavy vehicles, the observed subtotal is 254 motor vehicles. Multiplying by four gives an equivalent one-hour rate of 1,016 vehicles per hour, not a measured continuous-hour count. The distinction matters where arrivals come in bursts. Suppose the twelve buses carry an estimated 35 people each and the 180 cars average 1.4 people. These class-specific assumptions imply 420 people on buses and 252 in cars, before considering the motorcycles. A conclusion based only on vehicle volume would obscure the bus contribution to total passenger movement.\n\nSketch a detector location upstream of the railway crossing and another downstream. If the crossing frequently closes, the upstream detector may count vehicles arriving at a queue while the downstream detector measures constrained departures. Their different observations are not inconsistent. A queue evolution study needs synchronized times and a documented segment length. When reporting speed, distinguish a set of speeds collected at a detector from speed obtained by dividing corridor distance by measured travel time. A sample of fast-moving cars can dominate the observed time-mean speed while slower trucks dominate occupancy and shockwave behavior.\n\nThe ethical challenge is to preserve privacy. Video data may be useful for aggregate classifications but retaining visible faces and registration numbers is unnecessary for a classroom study. Prefer manually coded anonymous counts, legally collected publicly available observations or synthetic records. Record observation weather, local events, detector blind spots and the date on which the data were acquired. A comparison of survey days requires matching time-of-day and location before using it to attribute change to a new signal or road marking.\n\nFor an evaluation memo, propose two alternatives: improving traffic flow through signal coordination or protecting a bus crossing and pedestrian movement by reallocating space. Neither alternative should be declared superior without deciding what the corridor is intended to achieve. Calculate vehicle flow, estimated person flow and crossing delay as separate metrics. A strong recommendation names how each metric would be validated and identifies whose travel is omitted from the available observations."
