          "content": "## Lesson 2.1: Traffic counting and peak-hour factor\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nPermanent counters, short-duration manual counts and image detectors have different bias mechanisms. Peak-hour factor compares one-hour volume to the flow implied by its busiest subinterval. It captures peaking but does not correct detection errors or define demand suppressed by queue spillback.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nPHF = V_hour/(4V_15,max).\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nFour consecutive 15-minute counts of 200, 240, 310 and 250 vehicles give a one-hour volume of 1,000. The peak-hour factor is 1000/(4\u00d7310) = 0.806. A PHF below one indicates an uneven distribution of volume across quarters.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nExplain why a congested detector may show declining throughput as conditions worsen even while arrival demand increases.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nCount direction, lane, vehicle classification and weather. Never combine nonconcurrent 15-minute counts and label them a single observed hour.\n\n### Sensitivity and uncertainty\n\nVary one uncertain parameter while keeping others fixed, then vary a second parameter jointly. A steep response indicates that the result should be reported as a range rather than a single number. Do not equate a sensitivity exercise with a statistical confidence interval; uncertainty also includes model structure and measurement bias.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 2.1.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing an industrial connection used by large goods vehicles and irregular delivery convoys. The primary affected users include truck drivers and nearby residents. Before transferring the relationship PHF = V_hour/(4V_15,max). to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for reliable travel time, pavement damage and turning geometry; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on a freight-access management plan. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 2.1.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: Four consecutive 15-minute counts of 200, 240, 310 and 250 vehicles give a one-hour volume of 1,000. The peak-hour factor is 1000/(4\u00d7310) = 0.806. A PHF below one indicates an uneven distribution of volume across quarters. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 2.1.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Count direction, lane, vehicle classification and weather. Never combine nonconcurrent 15-minute counts and label them a single observed hour. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of traffic counting and peak-hour factor. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (PHF = V_hour/(4V_15,max).), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about traffic counting and peak-hour factor, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
