          "id": "sec-2-4",
          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Travel time, speed surveys and reliability",
          "content": "## Lesson 2.2: Travel time, speed surveys and reliability\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nA spot-speed survey observes passing vehicles, whereas corridor travel-time studies follow trips between endpoints. Travel-time reliability concerns variation between days and periods, not only the mean. Delay definitions depend on a selected reference time and can change simply because the benchmark changes.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nT = L/v, and planning time index = T_95/T_free when those travel times are consistently defined.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nAn 8 km corridor takes 12 minutes on a typical day but 21 minutes on the 95th-percentile day. With a 10-minute free-flow benchmark, the planning time index is 2.1; this is not a claim that ninety-five percent of drivers travel exactly 21 minutes.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nWhich percentile is relevant to emergency vehicles compared with a commuter who can vary departure time?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nSmall travel-time samples and GPS traces with poor map matching can create false precision in high percentiles.\n\n### Measurement protocol\n\nSpecify which observations distinguish the proposed engineering relationship from a coincidental correlation. Record station location, observation interval, vehicles or users included, calibration checks and missing data. A correct equation evaluated on a biased dataset can create a less defensible conclusion than a rough estimate accompanied by an explicit uncertainty interval.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 2.2.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a busy intersection surrounded by stores, loading areas and narrow footpaths. The primary affected users include local businesses, cyclists and walkers. Before transferring the relationship T = L/v, and planning time index = T_95/T_free when those travel times are consistently defined. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for conflicting curb use and vehicle queues; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on reassignment of curb space with inclusive access. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 2.2.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: An 8 km corridor takes 12 minutes on a typical day but 21 minutes on the 95th-percentile day. With a 10-minute free-flow benchmark, the planning time index is 2.1; this is not a claim that ninety-five percent of drivers travel exactly 21 minutes. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 2.2.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Small travel-time samples and GPS traces with poor map matching can create false precision in high percentiles. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of travel time, speed surveys and reliability. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (T = L/v, and planning time index = T_95/T_free when those travel times are consistently defined.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about travel time, speed surveys and reliability, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
