          "id": "sec-2-5",
          "secNumber": "2.5",
          "heading": "Capacity, bottlenecks and level of service",
          "content": "## Lesson 2.3: Capacity, bottlenecks and level of service\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nDemand, throughput and capacity are distinct quantities. A bottleneck may discharge fewer vehicles than arrive, building a queue. Lane-group saturation flow and multilane facility capacity depend on geometry, traffic composition, roadside friction and control. Level-of-service letter grades are framework-dependent and should not be equated with a universal measure of road success.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nv/c = demand/capacity for matched units and time periods.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nFor demand of 1,900 veh/h and measured throughput capacity of 1,600 veh/h, v/c = 1.1875. The excess arrival rate is approximately 300 veh/h during sustained oversaturation, provided demand and service remain roughly constant.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nWhat observations would tell you whether the corridor bottleneck is geometric, signal-controlled, incident-related or caused by a downstream queue?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nCapacity is not a single transferable constant. Use a location- and jurisdiction-appropriate method with calibrated inputs.\n\n### Dimensional audit\n\nBefore computing, rewrite every quantity in one coherent system of units. A time rate needs a time denominator; a spatial density needs a road-length denominator; a percentage must become a decimal before entering an algebraic equation. Unit checking can reject many plausible-looking but incorrect answers without any specialist software.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 2.3.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a developing area with uncertain future traffic and pedestrian demand. The primary affected users include new residents and existing communities. Before transferring the relationship v/c = demand/capacity for matched units and time periods. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for forecast error and long-lived capital decisions; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on phased investment with monitoring triggers. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 2.3.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: For demand of 1,900 veh/h and measured throughput capacity of 1,600 veh/h, v/c = 1.1875. The excess arrival rate is approximately 300 veh/h during sustained oversaturation, provided demand and service remain roughly constant. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 2.3.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Capacity is not a single transferable constant. Use a location- and jurisdiction-appropriate method with calibrated inputs. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of capacity, bottlenecks and level of service. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (v/c = demand/capacity for matched units and time periods.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about capacity, bottlenecks and level of service, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
