          "id": "sec-2-6",
          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Origin\u2013destination studies and sampling",
          "content": "## Lesson 2.4: Origin\u2013destination studies and sampling\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nTransport planning needs to understand who is traveling, from where, to where, when and by what mode. Roadside interviews, household surveys, smart cards, ticketing systems, mobile data and plate matching each observe only subsets of the travel market. Sampling weights can adjust measured groups, but cannot automatically correct missing or systematically excluded populations.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nExpanded trips in group i = observed sampled trips / sampling fraction_i.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nIf 30 sampled trips represent 10% of an eligible population, an expanded estimate is 300 trips for that specific sampling frame. Applying the same multiplier to every district is invalid when sampling rates and nonresponse differ.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nConstruct a minimal survey that can distinguish trips by purpose and mode without collecting unnecessary identifying personal information.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nPrivacy, consent, aggregation and unequal phone or internet access limit data use even when technical linkage is possible.\n\n### Alternative design comparison\n\nCompare at least two interventions against the same baseline. Record whether each changes demand, available capacity, exposure, pavement condition, human behavior or monitoring quality. An alternative with the largest improvement in one performance indicator is not necessarily superior when its cost, emissions, access impacts and failure modes are considered.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 2.4.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a two-lane route linking distant towns with limited passing opportunities. The primary affected users include intercity travelers and bus passengers. Before transferring the relationship Expanded trips in group i = observed sampled trips / sampling fraction_i. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for severe-crash exposure and overtaking constraints; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on targeted safety improvements rather than indiscriminate widening. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 2.4.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: If 30 sampled trips represent 10% of an eligible population, an expanded estimate is 300 trips for that specific sampling frame. Applying the same multiplier to every district is invalid when sampling rates and nonresponse differ. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 2.4.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Privacy, consent, aggregation and unequal phone or internet access limit data use even when technical linkage is possible. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of origin\u2013destination studies and sampling. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Expanded trips in group i = observed sampled trips / sampling fraction_i.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about origin\u2013destination studies and sampling, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
