          "content": "## Lesson 3.1: Design speed, stopping sight distance and grade\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nSight distance links driver perception, reaction and braking. The stopping process has a perception\u2013reaction segment followed by deceleration. A simple screening model assumes level ground or constant grade and uniform tire\u2013road friction; actual design uses adopted local standards and driver population assumptions.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nSSD = vt + v\u00b2/[2g(f \u00b1 G)] with v in m/s and grade G expressed as a decimal.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nAt 80 km/h (22.22 m/s), reaction time 2.5 s, friction f = 0.35 and level grade, reaction distance is 55.6 m and braking distance about 71.9 m, giving roughly 127.5 m. This calculation must not be treated as a legally sufficient roadway design value.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nExplore wet friction, downhill grade, night visibility and vehicle class; which changes would most affect stopping distance at high speed?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nA stopping-distance equation cannot replace geometric sight-line checks, weather-specific friction data or the relevant design-standard table.\n\n### Measurement protocol\n\nSpecify which observations distinguish the proposed engineering relationship from a coincidental correlation. Record station location, observation interval, vehicles or users included, calibration checks and missing data. A correct equation evaluated on a biased dataset can create a less defensible conclusion than a rough estimate accompanied by an explicit uncertainty interval.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 3.1.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a busy intersection surrounded by stores, loading areas and narrow footpaths. The primary affected users include local businesses, cyclists and walkers. Before transferring the relationship SSD = vt + v\u00b2/[2g(f \u00b1 G)] with v in m/s and grade G expressed as a decimal. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for conflicting curb use and vehicle queues; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on reassignment of curb space with inclusive access. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 3.1.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: At 80 km/h (22.22 m/s), reaction time 2.5 s, friction f = 0.35 and level grade, reaction distance is 55.6 m and braking distance about 71.9 m, giving roughly 127.5 m. This calculation must not be treated as a legally sufficient roadway design value. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 3.1.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: A stopping-distance equation cannot replace geometric sight-line checks, weather-specific friction data or the relevant design-standard table. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of design speed, stopping sight distance and grade. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (SSD = vt + v\u00b2/[2g(f \u00b1 G)] with v in m/s and grade G expressed as a decimal.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about design speed, stopping sight distance and grade, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant geometric decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
