          "id": "sec-3-4",
          "secNumber": "3.4",
          "heading": "Horizontal curves and superelevation",
          "content": "## Lesson 3.2: Horizontal curves and superelevation\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nA horizontal road curve redirects the vehicle. Lateral acceleration scales with the square of speed and inversely with radius; pavement crossfall helps balance lateral demand, but allowable side friction depends on driver comfort and safety assumptions. Curves also influence available sight distance through roadside obstructions.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nR = V\u00b2/[127(e+f)] using V in km/h, R in metres, e and f as fractions.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nAt 80 km/h with e = 0.06 and f = 0.10, the idealized balance yields R = 6400/(127\u00d70.16) \u2248 315 m. The maximum permitted e and design side-friction are jurisdiction-specific, not freely interchangeable knobs.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nWhat happens to the required radius if the design speed increases from 80 to 100 km/h without changing superelevation or friction?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nThe simplified formula omits runoff transitions, trucks with higher centres of gravity, curve widening and combined vertical alignment.\n\n### Dimensional audit\n\nBefore computing, rewrite every quantity in one coherent system of units. A time rate needs a time denominator; a spatial density needs a road-length denominator; a percentage must become a decimal before entering an algebraic equation. Unit checking can reject many plausible-looking but incorrect answers without any specialist software.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 3.2.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a developing area with uncertain future traffic and pedestrian demand. The primary affected users include new residents and existing communities. Before transferring the relationship R = V\u00b2/[127(e+f)] using V in km/h, R in metres, e and f as fractions. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for forecast error and long-lived capital decisions; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on phased investment with monitoring triggers. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 3.2.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: At 80 km/h with e = 0.06 and f = 0.10, the idealized balance yields R = 6400/(127\u00d70.16) \u2248 315 m. The maximum permitted e and design side-friction are jurisdiction-specific, not freely interchangeable knobs. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 3.2.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: The simplified formula omits runoff transitions, trucks with higher centres of gravity, curve widening and combined vertical alignment. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of horizontal curves and superelevation. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (R = V\u00b2/[127(e+f)] using V in km/h, R in metres, e and f as fractions.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about horizontal curves and superelevation, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant geometric decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
