          "id": "sec-3-6",
          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Cross sections, drainage and accessible streets",
          "content": "## Lesson 3.4: Cross sections, drainage and accessible streets\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nA roadway cross-section allocates width to travel lanes, shoulders, footpaths, bike facilities, medians and drainage. A crossfall drains water but may also create challenging slopes for wheelchairs and cyclists. Safe complete-street design is a constraint-balancing process, not simply maximizing the number of vehicle lanes.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nSurface lateral drop = crossfall \u00d7 width, with crossfall dimensionless.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nA 3.5 m travel lane with a 2.0% crossfall has a centre-to-edge drop of 70 mm if it falls across the full lane width. The result says nothing about whether the curb inlet has adequate capacity to remove the runoff.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nSketch a corridor that must accommodate a bus stop, an accessible footpath, drainage inlet and bicycle movement in a constrained right of way.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nDo not treat crossfall as a substitute for cross-section hydraulic analysis or local accessibility guidance.\n\n### Sensitivity and uncertainty\n\nVary one uncertain parameter while keeping others fixed, then vary a second parameter jointly. A steep response indicates that the result should be reported as a range rather than a single number. Do not equate a sensitivity exercise with a statistical confidence interval; uncertainty also includes model structure and measurement bias.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 3.4.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a road with crowded mixed traffic and a frequent bus service. The primary affected users include transit riders and general-road users. Before transferring the relationship Surface lateral drop = crossfall \u00d7 width, with crossfall dimensionless. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for person throughput and bus headway variability; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on transit priority coupled with safer stops. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 3.4.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: A 3.5 m travel lane with a 2.0% crossfall has a centre-to-edge drop of 70 mm if it falls across the full lane width. The result says nothing about whether the curb inlet has adequate capacity to remove the runoff. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 3.4.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Do not treat crossfall as a substitute for cross-section hydraulic analysis or local accessibility guidance. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of cross sections, drainage and accessible streets. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Surface lateral drop = crossfall \u00d7 width, with crossfall dimensionless.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about cross sections, drainage and accessible streets, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant geometric decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
