          "secNumber": "3.9",
          "heading": "Extended design exercise: a curved road beside a school and drainage canal",
          "content": "## Extended design exercise: a curved road beside a school and drainage canal\n\nA rural-to-urban transition approaches a school along a left-turning horizontal curve. The road carries buses and goods vehicles and runs beside a drainage canal. Students approach on foot from both sides. An initial geometric screening at 70 km/h with superelevation 0.05 and illustrative side-friction factor 0.10 gives a radius from R=V\u00b2/[127(e+f)] of about 257 metres. This figure is a calculated balance, not a permitted or sufficiently safe curve radius. The actual design depends on the adopted code, available sight distance, vehicles and road environment. The need to support pedestrians near a school is itself a reason to question whether the desired operating speed is appropriate.\n\nBefore proposing a larger radius, the engineer plots the existing horizontal curve using surveyed coordinates and reconstructs road edges, roadside canal boundaries and property entrances. The curve may be broad enough in plan but still have a hidden crossing behind a wall. A stopping sight-distance check must consider the driver's line of sight, grade and obstruction height. For a simplified level-road scenario at 70 km/h, reaction time 2.5 seconds and friction 0.35, reaction distance is about 48.6 metres and braking distance about 55.1 metres, making the approximate stopping distance 103.7 metres. This does not incorporate sight-line obstructions, headlight effects or local design-standard values.\n\nThe road profile may also include a crest. Draw a parabolic vertical curve whose initial and final tangent grades are known; do not confuse the ability to calculate its elevation with verification that a driver can see a child crossing ahead. If the roadway crest hides part of a crossing, a geometrically smooth profile is still hazardous. Similarly, a two-percent crossfall across a three-metre lane yields a vertical difference of 60 millimetres, but does not prove runoff will enter a working inlet. The canal-side drainage pattern needs an independent hydraulic and erosion review. Raising a shoulder could unintentionally divert storm runoff toward nearby houses.\n\nPropose alternatives: reducing the target speed and improving crossings; relocating an obstruction; revising the alignment; or providing a physically separated pedestrian route. The first three have different costs and environmental impacts. A lower-speed corridor can sometimes improve road-user safety at less expense than a major realignment, but that outcome must be measured, not assumed. Record peak pedestrian flows, bus stopping locations, wet-weather visibility, the location of vulnerable access points and observed operating speeds across several periods. Public consultation is necessary because the shortest walking route and the driver's preferred travel path may differ.\n\nFinal submission must include annotated plan and profile views, a table of assumptions with units, calculation of the screening radius and SSD, a list of omitted visibility checks and two alternative corridor designs. The final recommendation must be framed as a proposal for proper standards-based assessment, not a construction drawing. Explain how the conclusion changes if the desired design speed is reduced, the pavement is wet or a bus stop is added near the crossing."
