          "content": "## Lesson 4.1: Unsignalized intersections and gap acceptance\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nAt an unsignalized junction, drivers on minor approaches depend on usable gaps in conflicting traffic. Critical gap and follow-up time are behavioral parameters that can vary by location, intersection layout and vehicle type. A simplified analytical model estimates potential service but cannot automatically reflect sight obstruction, lane-by-lane interactions or pedestrian conflict.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nSimplified accepted gaps depend on arrival distribution and chosen critical-gap threshold.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nIf opposing traffic arrives at 600 veh/h, a mean spacing of six seconds might appear comfortable, but an exponential arrival model also permits many much shorter headways. A single average cannot determine the probability of an acceptable opportunity.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nCompare a short pedestrian crossing with a truck turning across several lanes: why will accepted-gap distributions differ?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nEmpirical critical-gap estimates require careful treatment of rejected gaps, truncated observations and driver heterogeneity.\n\n### Dimensional audit\n\nBefore computing, rewrite every quantity in one coherent system of units. A time rate needs a time denominator; a spatial density needs a road-length denominator; a percentage must become a decimal before entering an algebraic equation. Unit checking can reject many plausible-looking but incorrect answers without any specialist software.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 4.1.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a developing area with uncertain future traffic and pedestrian demand. The primary affected users include new residents and existing communities. Before transferring the relationship Simplified accepted gaps depend on arrival distribution and chosen critical-gap threshold. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for forecast error and long-lived capital decisions; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on phased investment with monitoring triggers. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 4.1.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: If opposing traffic arrives at 600 veh/h, a mean spacing of six seconds might appear comfortable, but an exponential arrival model also permits many much shorter headways. A single average cannot determine the probability of an acceptable opportunity. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 4.1.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Empirical critical-gap estimates require careful treatment of rejected gaps, truncated observations and driver heterogeneity. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of unsignalized intersections and gap acceptance. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Simplified accepted gaps depend on arrival distribution and chosen critical-gap threshold.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about unsignalized intersections and gap acceptance, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
