          "id": "sec-4-4",
          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "Signal phases, saturation flow and capacity",
          "content": "## Lesson 4.2: Signal phases, saturation flow and capacity\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nA fixed-time traffic signal allocates part of a cycle to each movement. Effective green can differ from displayed green because of start-up losses and end-of-green utilization. The discharge capacity of a lane group is approximately its saturation flow multiplied by effective green ratio.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nc = s(g/C) for saturation flow s, effective green g and cycle C.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nWith saturation flow 1,800 veh/h, effective green 32 s and cycle 90 s, capacity is 640 veh/h. With demand of 720 veh/h, v/c = 1.125 and a persistent queue is expected if those conditions continue.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nWhich improvement might have more value: reallocating green, modifying turning restrictions, or reducing demand through alternative access?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nThe simple capacity formula cannot capture shared turning lanes, heavy vehicle adjustment, spillback or actuated control dynamics.\n\n### Alternative design comparison\n\nCompare at least two interventions against the same baseline. Record whether each changes demand, available capacity, exposure, pavement condition, human behavior or monitoring quality. An alternative with the largest improvement in one performance indicator is not necessarily superior when its cost, emissions, access impacts and failure modes are considered.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 4.2.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a two-lane route linking distant towns with limited passing opportunities. The primary affected users include intercity travelers and bus passengers. Before transferring the relationship c = s(g/C) for saturation flow s, effective green g and cycle C. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for severe-crash exposure and overtaking constraints; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on targeted safety improvements rather than indiscriminate widening. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 4.2.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: With saturation flow 1,800 veh/h, effective green 32 s and cycle 90 s, capacity is 640 veh/h. With demand of 720 veh/h, v/c = 1.125 and a persistent queue is expected if those conditions continue. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 4.2.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: The simple capacity formula cannot capture shared turning lanes, heavy vehicle adjustment, spillback or actuated control dynamics. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of signal phases, saturation flow and capacity. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (c = s(g/C) for saturation flow s, effective green g and cycle C.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about signal phases, saturation flow and capacity, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
