          "heading": "Webster cycle estimation and lost time",
          "content": "## Lesson 4.3: Webster cycle estimation and lost time\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nWebster\u2019s cycle approximation is an analytical planning relation derived for fixed-time conditions with random arrivals. Lost time increases the fraction of each cycle that cannot carry traffic. The critical flow ratio sum must stay below one for a finite mathematical optimum; near unity the cycle estimate becomes extremely sensitive and may be operationally impractical.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nC\u2080 = (1.5L + 5)/(1 \u2212 Y), where L is seconds and Y is the sum of critical ratios.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nFor L = 12 s and Y = 0.76, the idealized cycle is (18+5)/0.24 = 95.8 s. If Y rises to 0.95, the estimate becomes 460 s, indicating a breakdown of the formula as a practical operational recommendation.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nTest the sensitivity of the estimate to \u00b10.05 in Y and explain why detector uncertainty near saturation is consequential.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nMinimum pedestrian clearance, progression, maximum queue storage and coordination must be checked separately.\n\n### Sensitivity and uncertainty\n\nVary one uncertain parameter while keeping others fixed, then vary a second parameter jointly. A steep response indicates that the result should be reported as a range rather than a single number. Do not equate a sensitivity exercise with a statistical confidence interval; uncertainty also includes model structure and measurement bias.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 4.3.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a road with crowded mixed traffic and a frequent bus service. The primary affected users include transit riders and general-road users. Before transferring the relationship C\u2080 = (1.5L + 5)/(1 \u2212 Y), where L is seconds and Y is the sum of critical ratios. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for person throughput and bus headway variability; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on transit priority coupled with safer stops. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 4.3.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: For L = 12 s and Y = 0.76, the idealized cycle is (18+5)/0.24 = 95.8 s. If Y rises to 0.95, the estimate becomes 460 s, indicating a breakdown of the formula as a practical operational recommendation. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 4.3.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Minimum pedestrian clearance, progression, maximum queue storage and coordination must be checked separately. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of webster cycle estimation and lost time. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (C\u2080 = (1.5L + 5)/(1 \u2212 Y), where L is seconds and Y is the sum of critical ratios.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about webster cycle estimation and lost time, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant operational decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
