          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Maintenance, condition and lifecycle decisions",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.4: Maintenance, condition and lifecycle decisions\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nCondition surveys describe cracking, rut depth, roughness, skid resistance and drainage. A condition index is a management tool, not a physical law. The timing of maintenance matters because small interventions on a sound road can be more effective than waiting until full reconstruction is unavoidable.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nPresent value PV = \u03a3 cost_t/(1+r)^t when costs occur at year t.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nA road agency compares an immediate treatment of 1 million cost units with a 1.3 million treatment due in five years. At 5% annual discount, the future cost has PV \u2248 1.019 million. This simple comparison ignores performance, residual value and user delays.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nCreate a lifecycle decision that includes construction emission, user delay, periodic maintenance and climate risk instead of only agency cost.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nUse real condition observations and consistent analysis horizons. A lower discounted agency cost need not mean a socially preferable outcome.\n\n### Dimensional audit\n\nBefore computing, rewrite every quantity in one coherent system of units. A time rate needs a time denominator; a spatial density needs a road-length denominator; a percentage must become a decimal before entering an algebraic equation. Unit checking can reject many plausible-looking but incorrect answers without any specialist software.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 5.4.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a low-lying corridor with intermittent drainage blockage after intense storms. The primary affected users include commuters, merchants and emergency responders. Before transferring the relationship Present value PV = \u03a3 cost_t/(1+r)^t when costs occur at year t. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for flooded travel lanes and unreliable service; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on drainage maintenance plus a safe diversion strategy. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 5.4.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: A road agency compares an immediate treatment of 1 million cost units with a 1.3 million treatment due in five years. At 5% annual discount, the future cost has PV \u2248 1.019 million. This simple comparison ignores performance, residual value and user delays. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 5.4.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Use real condition observations and consistent analysis horizons. A lower discounted agency cost need not mean a socially preferable outcome. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of maintenance, condition and lifecycle decisions. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Present value PV = \u03a3 cost_t/(1+r)^t when costs occur at year t.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about maintenance, condition and lifecycle decisions, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant infrastructure decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
