          "id": "sec-5-9",
          "secNumber": "5.9",
          "heading": "Extended pavement case: a freight corridor with seasonal flooding",
          "content": "## Extended pavement case: a freight corridor with seasonal flooding\n\nA highway carrying heavy trucks develops wheel-path rutting and cracking one year after major resurfacing. A simple design memo initially attributes the problem to unexpectedly high axle loads and requests thicker asphalt. A stronger investigation compares truck counts, axle configurations, aggregate quality, density tests, subgrade moisture and surface drainage before deciding on the repair. Rutting in the asphalt layer, rutting in the unbound base and deformation in the subgrade can look similar at the pavement surface but require different corrective actions. Cores, deflection measurements and trench observations help locate the deforming layer.\n\nThe fourth-power relationship is useful for demonstrating the possibility of disproportionate heavy-vehicle damage. With a reference axle of 80 kN, an axle of 120 kN has a damage index of 1.5^4 = 5.0625 under that specific mathematical approximation. An axle of 160 kN gives sixteen times the reference index. These are not calibrated equivalent single-axle load factors and cannot by themselves establish pavement life. Different axle groups and pavement failure mechanisms need appropriate design factors. Repeating one rough exponent for every vehicle and road can substantially distort a project forecast.\n\nGroundwater observations may be equally important. Inspect edge drains, outlets, sealed shoulders, culverts and seasonal water-table elevation. Wet granular base layers can lose stiffness; poor drainage can increase pumping or moisture damage. Survey crossfall and ponding locations during storms rather than assuming the road is well drained because it looks dry during a site visit. Short-lived resurfacing gains often reflect treating symptoms without correcting moisture or structural failure. Construction records should establish whether specified compaction and binder quality were achieved.\n\nA rigid concrete option changes the problem, rather than eliminating it. With ideal plate rigidity proportional to the cube of slab thickness, increasing thickness from 200 to 220 millimetres increases elastic plate stiffness by a factor (220/200)^3 \u2248 1.331 if modulus and Poisson ratio remain constant. It does not imply a 33% improvement in fatigue life or durability. Joints, load transfer, thermal gradients, soil support and water under the slab must still be evaluated.\n\nCompare three strategies: drainage rehabilitation with local base repairs, strengthened flexible pavement and a reconstructed concrete pavement. Use an equal analysis horizon and include construction costs, traffic disruption, periodic maintenance, embodied emissions, heavy vehicle demand uncertainty and probable storm exposure. A cheapest-initial-cost decision may transfer risk to future road users and maintenance budgets. A reconstruction option could also impose significant present-day access costs on businesses. Explain the evidence that would cause you to change your preferred option.\n\nYour project report should contain a distress map, a targeted sampling plan, a table distinguishing damage mechanisms from visual symptoms, a transparent axle sensitivity graph and a lifecycle comparison. Do not claim that the simplified 16-fold damage value predicts actual service life. Document exactly which elements require a jurisdictionally adopted mechanistic\u2013empirical or other approved pavement design method."
