          "heading": "Public transport capacity, frequency and reliability",
          "content": "## Lesson 7.1: Public transport capacity, frequency and reliability\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nService frequency and vehicle capacity determine nominal passenger-carrying ability, but dwell times, congestion and schedule variation affect delivered service. A bus corridor carrying fewer vehicles can move more people than a general traffic lane. Passenger volume measures access differently from traffic throughput.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nDirectional passenger capacity = vehicles/hour \u00d7 usable passengers/vehicle.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nA bus route with one bus every eight minutes offers 7.5 buses per hour. At 60 usable passengers per bus, nominal directional capacity is 450 passengers/h. Actual boardings depend on travel patterns and stop loads.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nCompare strategies of longer vehicles, higher frequency and transit priority for a crowded route with unreliable operation.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nNominal capacity does not equal passenger demand; safety limits, access at stops and bus bunching constrain deployment.\n\n### Measurement protocol\n\nSpecify which observations distinguish the proposed engineering relationship from a coincidental correlation. Record station location, observation interval, vehicles or users included, calibration checks and missing data. A correct equation evaluated on a biased dataset can create a less defensible conclusion than a rough estimate accompanied by an explicit uncertainty interval.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 7.1.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a school frontage where crossing demand varies sharply during arrival and dismissal. The primary affected users include students and caregivers. Before transferring the relationship Directional passenger capacity = vehicles/hour \u00d7 usable passengers/vehicle. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for crossing delay, visibility and conflict; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on speed management and a protected walking route. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 7.1.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: A bus route with one bus every eight minutes offers 7.5 buses per hour. At 60 usable passengers per bus, nominal directional capacity is 450 passengers/h. Actual boardings depend on travel patterns and stop loads. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 7.1.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Nominal capacity does not equal passenger demand; safety limits, access at stops and bus bunching constrain deployment. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of public transport capacity, frequency and reliability. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Directional passenger capacity = vehicles/hour \u00d7 usable passengers/vehicle.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about public transport capacity, frequency and reliability, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant transportation-system decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
