          "id": "sec-7-4",
          "secNumber": "7.4",
          "heading": "Transit travel times and stop spacing",
          "content": "## Lesson 7.2: Transit travel times and stop spacing\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nStop spacing trades accessibility against running time. Close stops shorten walking distances but add acceleration, braking and passenger dwell penalties. Bus travel time combines cruise time, stop dwell, signal delay and stochastic congestion. End-to-end speed can be much lower than cruise speed.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nT = L/v_run + N(t_dwell+t_accel) + t_delay for an approximate service segment.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nA 6 km bus segment at moving speed 30 km/h takes 12 minutes before stops. If ten stops add 25 s dwell and 10 s acceleration delay each, stop penalties add 5.83 minutes, excluding traffic signals.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nWhy may a stop-spacing improvement help average journey times yet harm travelers who cannot walk long distances?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nAssuming every scheduled stop is used or every bus experiences the same delay may overestimate or underestimate real service.\n\n### Dimensional audit\n\nBefore computing, rewrite every quantity in one coherent system of units. A time rate needs a time denominator; a spatial density needs a road-length denominator; a percentage must become a decimal before entering an algebraic equation. Unit checking can reject many plausible-looking but incorrect answers without any specialist software.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 7.2.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a low-lying corridor with intermittent drainage blockage after intense storms. The primary affected users include commuters, merchants and emergency responders. Before transferring the relationship T = L/v_run + N(t_dwell+t_accel) + t_delay for an approximate service segment. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for flooded travel lanes and unreliable service; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on drainage maintenance plus a safe diversion strategy. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 7.2.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: A 6 km bus segment at moving speed 30 km/h takes 12 minutes before stops. If ten stops add 25 s dwell and 10 s acceleration delay each, stop penalties add 5.83 minutes, excluding traffic signals. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 7.2.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Assuming every scheduled stop is used or every bus experiences the same delay may overestimate or underestimate real service. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of transit travel times and stop spacing. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (T = L/v_run + N(t_dwell+t_accel) + t_delay for an approximate service segment.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about transit travel times and stop spacing, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant transportation-system decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
