          "id": "sec-7-5",
          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Pedestrian crossing and accessible design",
          "content": "## Lesson 7.3: Pedestrian crossing and accessible design\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nPedestrian crossing performance depends on walking distance, waiting time, visibility, physical accessibility and conflict exposure. A fixed clearance interval derived from a single walking speed may fail to accommodate users with limited mobility. Curb ramps, tactile guidance and continuous paths are integral rather than optional.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nIllustrative crossing time = width / walking speed.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nAt a crossing width of 18 m and a walking speed of 1.0 m/s, movement time is 18 s. This excludes start-up time and any additional jurisdictional requirements for clearance and accessibility.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nHow would an accessible refuge island change the geometry and risk of a wide crossing where vehicle volumes are heavy?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nDo not use this division alone to set legal signal clearance intervals; follow the adopted traffic-control and accessibility standards.\n\n### Alternative design comparison\n\nCompare at least two interventions against the same baseline. Record whether each changes demand, available capacity, exposure, pavement condition, human behavior or monitoring quality. An alternative with the largest improvement in one performance indicator is not necessarily superior when its cost, emissions, access impacts and failure modes are considered.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 7.3.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a bridge rehabilitation requiring temporary lane closures. The primary affected users include construction teams and road users. Before transferring the relationship Illustrative crossing time = width / walking speed. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for unexpected queues and disrupted access; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on staged works and active travel-time messaging. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 7.3.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: At a crossing width of 18 m and a walking speed of 1.0 m/s, movement time is 18 s. This excludes start-up time and any additional jurisdictional requirements for clearance and accessibility. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 7.3.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Do not use this division alone to set legal signal clearance intervals; follow the adopted traffic-control and accessibility standards. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of pedestrian crossing and accessible design. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Illustrative crossing time = width / walking speed.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about pedestrian crossing and accessible design, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant transportation-system decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
