          "id": "sec-7-6",
          "secNumber": "7.6",
          "heading": "Bicycle networks and multimodal safety",
          "content": "## Lesson 7.4: Bicycle networks and multimodal safety\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nA cycling network requires route continuity, safe conflict management and usable intersections, not isolated short segments. Separation, speed management, crossing design and maintenance influence comfort and safety. Network completeness can be a stronger accessibility measure than a single facility width.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nBicycle trip time T = route length/mean moving speed plus intersection delay.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nA 4.5 km protected bicycle route traveled at 15 km/h requires 18 minutes moving time. Five crossings averaging 20 s of delay add about 1.67 minutes. The route may still outperform a shorter but dangerous network gap.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nCompare perceived safety and actual conflict exposure across protected lanes, shared streets and painted lanes with similar geometric widths.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nTravel times alone are insufficient to evaluate mode choice; intersection quality and personal security matter.\n\n### Sensitivity and uncertainty\n\nVary one uncertain parameter while keeping others fixed, then vary a second parameter jointly. A steep response indicates that the result should be reported as a range rather than a single number. Do not equate a sensitivity exercise with a statistical confidence interval; uncertainty also includes model structure and measurement bias.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 7.4.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing an industrial connection used by large goods vehicles and irregular delivery convoys. The primary affected users include truck drivers and nearby residents. Before transferring the relationship Bicycle trip time T = route length/mean moving speed plus intersection delay. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for reliable travel time, pavement damage and turning geometry; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on a freight-access management plan. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 7.4.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: A 4.5 km protected bicycle route traveled at 15 km/h requires 18 minutes moving time. Five crossings averaging 20 s of delay add about 1.67 minutes. The route may still outperform a shorter but dangerous network gap. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 7.4.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Travel times alone are insufficient to evaluate mode choice; intersection quality and personal security matter. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of bicycle networks and multimodal safety. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Bicycle trip time T = route length/mean moving speed plus intersection delay.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about bicycle networks and multimodal safety, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant transportation-system decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
