          "content": "## Lesson 8.1: Crash data, exposure and regression to the mean\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nCrash counts must be interpreted alongside exposure, severity, reporting bias and random variation. A site with unusually high crashes one year may naturally decline even without treatment. Before\u2013after evaluation can therefore overstate improvements when a location is selected because of an exceptionally bad recent period.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nIllustrative crash rate per million vehicle-km = crashes \u00d7 10\u2076/(AADT \u00d7 365 \u00d7 length_km).\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nFor six reported crashes, AADT 12,000 and a 2 km segment observed for one year, the estimated rate is 6e6/(12000\u00d7365\u00d72) \u2248 0.685 crashes per million vehicle-km. It says nothing about injury severity or statistical uncertainty.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nHow would underreporting or different road-user composition affect comparisons of two apparently similar sites?\n\n### Important limitations\n\nThe calculation is descriptive; it is not a predictive crash model or causal measure of engineering countermeasure effectiveness.\n\n### Dimensional audit\n\nBefore computing, rewrite every quantity in one coherent system of units. A time rate needs a time denominator; a spatial density needs a road-length denominator; a percentage must become a decimal before entering an algebraic equation. Unit checking can reject many plausible-looking but incorrect answers without any specialist software.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 8.1.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a low-lying corridor with intermittent drainage blockage after intense storms. The primary affected users include commuters, merchants and emergency responders. Before transferring the relationship Illustrative crash rate per million vehicle-km = crashes \u00d7 10\u2076/(AADT \u00d7 365 \u00d7 length_km). to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for flooded travel lanes and unreliable service; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on drainage maintenance plus a safe diversion strategy. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 8.1.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: For six reported crashes, AADT 12,000 and a 2 km segment observed for one year, the estimated rate is 6e6/(12000\u00d7365\u00d72) \u2248 0.685 crashes per million vehicle-km. It says nothing about injury severity or statistical uncertainty. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 8.1.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: The calculation is descriptive; it is not a predictive crash model or causal measure of engineering countermeasure effectiveness. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of crash data, exposure and regression to the mean. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Illustrative crash rate per million vehicle-km = crashes \u00d7 10\u2076/(AADT \u00d7 365 \u00d7 length_km).), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about crash data, exposure and regression to the mean, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant transportation-system decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
