          "id": "sec-8-6",
          "secNumber": "8.6",
          "heading": "Integrated corridor planning and climate resilience",
          "content": "## Lesson 8.4: Integrated corridor planning and climate resilience\n\n### Physical and planning meaning\n\nA transport corridor links traffic operations, road structure, drainage, emissions, ecology, land use and access. The cheapest initial option can be fragile in extreme rain or incompatible with an expanding walking network. Scenario analysis tests alternatives against several plausible futures rather than selecting a single forecast as certain.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\nExpected annual loss = \u03a3 probability_i \u00d7 consequence_i for a well-defined set of mutually exclusive scenarios.\n\n### Interpreted worked case\n\nSuppose an annual flood probability is 5% and estimated direct repair loss is 20 million cost units if the event occurs. The simple expected annual direct loss is one million before indirect disruption, recovery time and correlated hazards.\n\n### Investigating an assumption\n\nChoose two resilient alternatives and explain how they perform under both normal demand growth and a more extreme precipitation scenario.\n\n### Important limitations\n\nExpected values do not capture tail risk, equity, emergency access or the long life of infrastructure without deeper analysis.\n\n### Measurement protocol\n\nSpecify which observations distinguish the proposed engineering relationship from a coincidental correlation. Record station location, observation interval, vehicles or users included, calibration checks and missing data. A correct equation evaluated on a biased dataset can create a less defensible conclusion than a rough estimate accompanied by an explicit uncertainty interval.\n\n### Three distinct evidence-based investigations\n\n#### Investigation 8.4.1\n\nAn agency is reviewing a busy intersection surrounded by stores, loading areas and narrow footpaths. The primary affected users include local businesses, cyclists and walkers. Before transferring the relationship Expected annual loss = \u03a3 probability_i \u00d7 consequence_i for a well-defined set of mutually exclusive scenarios. to the location, explain which inputs could be directly observed, which would need a proxy, and which are not meaningful without a local policy or design standard. Establish a baseline for conflicting curb use and vehicle queues; compare that baseline with an alternative centred on reassignment of curb space with inclusive access. Your written answer must separate improvements that follow from a formula from improvements that are only plausible hypotheses. Address conditions in which the intervention would fail.\n\n#### Investigation 8.4.2\n\nUse this worked reference as a challenge rather than as a template to memorize: Suppose an annual flood probability is 5% and estimated direct repair loss is 20 million cost units if the event occurs. The simple expected annual direct loss is one million before indirect disruption, recovery time and correlated hazards. Replace one input with a plausible field observation, state why the replacement is plausible, and predict the direction of the result before computing. If the prediction differs from the calculation, investigate units and the sign convention first. Explain how uncertainty from measurement, behavior and a simplified model affects the credibility of a precise numerical answer.\n\n#### Investigation 8.4.3\n\nA project manager proposes to use a single number from this topic to approve an investment. Prepare a critique rooted in the following limitation: Expected values do not capture tail risk, equity, emergency access or the long life of infrastructure without deeper analysis. List two measurements that would improve the study, one externality that the calculation does not represent and one practical decision that could be deferred until the missing evidence is obtained. Avoid claiming the educational model has been professionally calibrated or validated on a real transportation network.\n\n### Explain the computation to a classmate\n\nWithout looking at the numerical illustration, reconstruct the logic of integrated corridor planning and climate resilience. Start with the field measurement and unit definition, define the reference condition, state the idealization in the equation (Expected annual loss = \u03a3 probability_i \u00d7 consequence_i for a well-defined set of mutually exclusive scenarios.), and then walk through the calculation so that a reader can independently check every value. Identify which result can be checked by a second technique. If observations contradict the simple model, explain how you would revise the model rather than discard the observations.\n\n### Field data and reporting protocol\n\nFor a short field exercise about integrated corridor planning and climate resilience, specify a time window, observation location, data dictionary and recording method. Protect individual privacy by avoiding unnecessary vehicle or personal identifiers. Use at least three quality checks: plausibility ranges for the raw input, missing-value counts, and a reconciliation of any aggregated totals. Present separate notes on systematic bias, day-to-day variability and uncertainty in the equation itself.\n\n### Decision boundary\n\nThe relationship in this lesson is appropriate for concept exploration and transparent back-of-the-envelope screening. A code-compliant transportation-system decision would additionally need jurisdiction-specific requirements, evidence of relevant site conditions and a documented professional review. Name the exact assumptions that would need replacement and identify stakeholders who might be excluded if the decision were made entirely from a vehicle-centred metric."
