          "id": "sec-8-9",
          "secNumber": "8.9",
          "heading": "Extended safety and appraisal case: a high-crash corridor",
          "content": "## Extended safety and appraisal case: a high-crash corridor\n\nA regional road agency notices that a 2-kilometre corridor has six reported crashes over the past year. Daily traffic volume is estimated at 12,000 vehicles. A descriptive rate is 6 \u00d7 10^6/(12,000 \u00d7 365 \u00d7 2) \u2248 0.685 crashes per million vehicle-kilometres. Such a rate can help organize exposure information, but comparisons require consistent injury reporting, segment definitions, traffic measurement and time windows. The agency should not rank a pedestrian-dense town-centre road against a limited-access motorway as if they are exchangeable sites with identical risk exposure.\n\nThe agency proposes to fund a guardrail because the corridor experienced unusually many crashes in the selection year. There is a selection-bias problem: choosing sites at their observed maximum tends to create apparent improvements when subsequent observations move toward ordinary conditions. Regression to the mean is not a defense for doing nothing; it is a reason to evaluate interventions properly. If an illustrative gamma prior for a yearly crash rate has mean two and effective prior exposure two site-years, observing three crashes in one new year yields posterior mean (4+3)/(2+1)=2.33 crashes/year. This toy model illustrates shrinkage but does not account for variable traffic exposure or roadway characteristics. An actual empirical Bayes analysis needs an appropriate safety performance function and reference-site data.\n\nCollect the circumstances of reported crashes, injury severity, turning movements, speed distribution, road-user types, lighting, road-edge conditions and relevant exposure. A higher share of serious crashes may justify action even when a simple total-count comparison does not rank the site highest. Assess the validity of police reports and hospital-linked records under local privacy and consent requirements; reporting rates can differ by road-user type. Build a collision diagram rather than relying only on a coloured dot map. Identify hazardous mechanisms that could be altered by engineering action.\n\nCompare interventions targeting the identified risk: speed management, a protected crossing, better junction sight lines, shoulder improvement, forgiving roadside design or enforcement in conjunction with engineering changes. Each has a different causal pathway. A treatment does not have a credible expected crash reduction merely because its name sounds safety-related. Research or locally validated crash modification factors are required for formal benefit estimates. Treat site-specific applicability, implementation fidelity and maintenance as part of the evaluation.\n\nFor a broader project, suppose an investment costs ten million cost units at the start, while an estimate of monetized benefit is 1.6 million per year for eight years. At discount rate 5%, the present value of that constant annuity is 1.6 \u00d7 [1\u2212(1.05)^\u22128]/0.05 \u2248 10.34 million. The resulting NPV is around 0.34 million before omitted items. The difference is small relative to plausible uncertainty in construction cost or forecast benefits. Sensitivity analysis and distributional impacts should therefore be central, not an afterthought.\n\nA credible corridor decision also tests flood resilience, freight routing, emergency access, school crossings and induced demand. Present a balanced dashboard that separates economic value, severe-injury risk, accessibility impacts and who pays. State how recommendations would change if crash underreporting is large, if a new school increases pedestrian exposure, or if a flood-prone bridge experiences more frequent closures. The purpose of the exercise is to improve decision quality under uncertainty, not to turn a simple calculator output into a claim of proven safety."
